VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-016
Reason for Visit: Acute severe lumbar back pain radiating to the legs and new-onset lower limb weakness requiring immediate neurological assessment to rule out malignant spinal cord compression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, unremitting bone pain and sudden, progressive bilateral leg weakness in a patient with a history of lower urinary tract symptoms. Urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally mandated to perform an immediate neurological examination, as malignant spinal cord compression is a time-critical oncological emergency. Delaying this assessment until regular in-hours consultation posed an unacceptable risk of permanent paraplegia and irreversible sphincter dysfunction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back is in absolute agony, and my legs suddenly feel completely numb and too weak to hold my weight."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have evolved over several months, beginning with progressive lower urinary tract symptoms, including a weak urinary stream, hesitancy, and nocturia. Over the past three weeks, the patient developed severe, unremitting bone pain localized to the lumbar spine and pelvis, which acutely worsened tonight. Concurrent with the pain exacerbation, they noted sudden weakness in their lower extremities. The patient also reports recent macroscopic haematuria, profound anorexia, unexplained unintentional weight loss of seven kilograms, and severe generalized fatigue with progressive lethargy over the past two months.
Functional Status: Acutely non-ambulatory due to new-onset lower extremity weakness and severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high fall risk; unable to safely mobilize to the bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.9C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Cachectic, pale, appearing chronically unwell and in acute, severe distress secondary to bone pain.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Digital rectal examination reveals a hard, nodular, and highly irregular prostate gland fixed to surrounding tissues. Neurological: Bilateral lower extremity weakness with 3/5 power in proximal muscle groups and diminished deep tendon reflexes, indicating impending spinal cord compression. Cardiovascular: Bilateral lower extremity oedema present, likely due to pelvic lymph node involvement. Expected investigation results include a markedly elevated serum prostate-specific antigen typically exceeding 100 ng/mL, accompanied by dense osteoblastic metastases on a radionuclide bone scan.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying on the floor, unable to rise following a minor slip caused by leg weakness.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; trial of paracetamol provided zero relief for the bone pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: High Risk Prostate Cancer - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of a hard, nodular prostate on examination and the presence of new neurological deficits.

Osteoporotic Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the concurrent severe lower urinary tract symptoms, haematuria, and abnormal prostate examination.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely afebrile presentation and the insidious, consumptive history of weight loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical picture of severe, unremitting lumbar pain, new-onset paraparesis, cachexia, and a hard, nodular prostate unequivocally points to advanced, metastatic prostate cancer with impending malignant spinal cord compression. This represents a true oncological and neurosurgical emergency. Urgent physical and neurological evaluation in the home was essential to definitively identify the severe motor deficits. Attempting to delay assessment until regular daytime general practice hours would constitute severe clinical negligence, as permanent spinal cord infarction and irreversible paraplegia occur rapidly without immediate high-dose corticosteroids and emergent surgical decompression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of oral Dexamethasone 16mg stat to urgently reduce peritumoral spinal oedema. Arranged priority emergency ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary hospital for emergent MRI of the neuroaxis and neurosurgical intervention.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate accurate pain tracking during their impending hospital admission, the patient is instructed to request analgesia immediately when their pain reaches a 4 out of 10, rather than waiting until it becomes unbearable. By experiencing rapid, effective pain control from early reporting, the patient will naturally build the habit of consistent, proactive symptom communication.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Direct medical handover provided to the receiving Emergency Department physician.
DME/Safety (Optional): None, patient requires immediate transport.
Follow-up: General practice follow-up is deferred to the hospital oncology and neurosurgical teams.

